-    "content": "# [Informational] Zero-Trust Security Architecture and Explicit Access Visibility\n\n## Overview\n\nSecurity is Priority 0 in \ud83d\udc4dkanban. Rather than relying on fragile application-level checks or leaky implicit permissions, \ud83d\udc4dkanban implements an uncompromising zero-trust architecture. Every request, query, and agent invocation is authenticated, scoped, and strictly evaluated against mathematical access invariants.\n\nKey security pillars:\n1. **Hardware-Enforced Database RLS:** PostgreSQL `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` is enabled across 100% of tenant tables.\n2. **Zero-Context Fail-Closed Execution:** Unbound database sessions return zero rows.\n3. **Explicit Access Visibility:** Every ticket, doc, and sticky renders an explicit sharing manifest with zero cross-tenant enumeration.\n4. **Agent On-Behalf-Of (OBO) Key Scoping:** Agent permissions are computed strictly as the mathematical intersection of the calling user's permissions and the API key's scope.\n5. **MCP Read-Taint High-Water Mark Defense:** Prevents autonomous agents from exfiltrating sensitive data read from higher-privilege scopes into lower-privilege write targets.\n\n---\n\n## Universal Access Control Model\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban unifies human users, user groups, and autonomous agent service-principals under a single Universal Object Graph access model.\n\n### Effective Resource Access (ERA) Evaluation\n\nAccess to any resource (`Project`, `Issue`, `Doc`, `Sticky`, `View`) is determined dynamically:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    User[\"Principal (Human / Agent)\"] --> Inherited[\"Inherited RBAC (Workspace & Project Roles)\"]\n    User --> Direct[\"Direct Resource ACLs (Allow & Deny Masks)\"]\n    Inherited --> Combined[\"Effective User Access = (Inherited \u222a Allow) \\ Deny\"]\n    \n    subgraph AgentOBO[\"Agent On-Behalf-Of Clamping\"]\n        Combined --> Intersect{\"Intersection (\u2229)\"}\n        KeyScope[\"API Key Scope Constraints<br/>(Allowed surfaces, projects, tools)\"] --> Intersect\n        AgentGrant[\"Explicit Agent Grant\"] --> Intersect\n        Intersect --> Effective[\"Final Effective Agent Permission\"]\n    end\n```\n\nWhere:\n- **Roles:** `Owner`, `Admin`, `Member`, `Guest`.\n- **Authoritative 8-Bit Capability Bitmask (`Capability(IntFlag)`):**\n  * `VIEW` (`0x01` / `1`): Read ticket summary, description, and metadata.\n  * `COMMENT` (`0x02` / `2`): Add comments to discussions and activity feeds.\n  * `EDIT` (`0x04` / `4`): Mutate scalar fields, tags, and assignments.\n  * `TRANSITION` (`0x08` / `8`): Move card state across workflow stages.\n  * `LINK` (`0x10` / `16`): Create or sever typed object links.\n  * `SHARE` (`0x20` / `32`): Modify access audience and direct object sharing.\n  * `ADMIN` (`0x40` / `64`): Manage permissions, scripted fields, and audit settings.\n  * `DELETE` (`0x80` / `128`): Soft-trash or permanently purge entities.\n  * `ALL` (`0xFF` / `255`): Full administrative capability mask.\n- **`SCOPE_CAPABILITY_MAP`:** Maps OAuth/API key scopes to capabilities:\n  * `session`: `Capability.ALL` (`0xFF`)\n  * `read`, `agent:read`, `docs:read`, `admin:read`: `Capability.VIEW` (`0x01`)\n  * `comment`: `Capability.VIEW | Capability.COMMENT` (`0x03`)\n  * `write`: `Capability.VIEW | Capability.COMMENT | Capability.EDIT | Capability.TRANSITION | Capability.LINK` (`0x1F`)\n  * `agent:write`: `Capability.VIEW | Capability.COMMENT | Capability.EDIT | Capability.TRANSITION | Capability.LINK` (`0x1F`)\n  * `docs:write`: `Capability.VIEW | Capability.COMMENT | Capability.EDIT | Capability.LINK` (`0x17`)\n  * `admin:write`: `Capability.ALL` (`0xFF`)\n- **Agent OBO Key Intersection:** When an autonomous agent acts on behalf of a user using an API key, the effective permission is clamped:\n  ```\n  AgentEffectiveAccess = UserPermissions INTERSECT KeyScopeConstraints\n  ```\n  An agent cannot escalate privileges beyond the user who generated the key, nor can it exceed the explicit scope configured on the key.\n\n---\n\n## PostgreSQL FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY (100% Coverage)\n\nEvery tenant table in PostgreSQL enforces Row-Level Security:\n\n```sql\nALTER TABLE issues ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nALTER TABLE issues FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\n```\n\n### The Fail-Closed Context Invariant\n\nPostgreSQL sessions authenticate as the unprivileged database role `kanban_app`. This role is stripped of `SUPERUSER` and `BYPASSRLS`. To query or mutate tenant data, the application server must establish an explicit session context within a transaction:\n\n```sql\nSET LOCAL app.current_principal_id = '01932e6b-7341-789a-bc01-123456789abc';\nSET LOCAL app.current_workspace_id = '01932e6b-7341-789a-bc01-def012345678';\n```\n\nIf a developer writes a raw query without setting the session context, the RLS policies fail closed: **zero rows are returned, and zero mutations succeed**. Even if an attacker executes SQL injection, they cannot bypass RLS to read cross-tenant data.\n\n---\n\n## Explicit Access Visibility and Sharing Manifests\n\nSaaS platforms often hide sharing rules behind obscure settings menus, leading to accidental leaks. In \ud83d\udc4dkanban, every resource view exposes a dynamic `\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f` sharing pill.\n\nClicking the sharing pill reveals the **Access Manifest**:\n- **Explicit Principals:** Lists all users, groups, and autonomous agents with direct access.\n- **Inherited Scope:** Displays parent project and workspace inheritance paths.\n- **Auditable Effective Mask:** Shows exact read, write, share, and delete capabilities for each principal.\n\n![Access Visibility Inspector and Sharing Manifest](images/17-access-visibility-inspector.png)\n\n### Anti-Enumeration and Timing Defense\n\nTo prevent malicious actors from scanning IDs or usernames:\n- **Identical RFC 7807 404 Responses:** Accessing an unauthorized resource returns an identical `404 Not Found` response as a non-existent resource. Unauthorized users cannot determine whether an object exists.\n- **25ms Timing Floor:** All resource lookups enforce a synthetic 25ms timing floor to neutralize side-channel timing attacks that measure database index lookups.\n\n---\n\n## MCP Read-Taint High-Water Mark Defense\n\nAutonomous LLMs can be tricked by prompt injection into reading confidential documents and writing excerpts into public tickets. \ud83d\udc4dkanban prevents this through **Read-Taint Tracking**:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Agent as Autonomous Agent\n    participant Gateway as MCP Gateway Taint Engine\n    participant PrivateDoc as Confidential Doc (Tier: PRIVATE)\n    participant PublicTicket as Public Ticket (Tier: PUBLIC)\n\n    Agent->>Gateway: read_doc(id='confidential_spec')\n    Gateway->>PrivateDoc: Fetch Document\n    PrivateDoc-->>Gateway: Return Content\n    Gateway->>Gateway: Elevate Agent Session High-Water Mark to PRIVATE\n    Gateway-->>Agent: Deliver Markdown Payload\n\n    Note over Agent: Later in conversation...\n    Agent->>Gateway: update_ticket(id='public_bug', body=...)\n    Gateway->>Gateway: Check Destination Scope (PUBLIC) vs High-Water Mark (PRIVATE)\n    Gateway-->>Agent: 403 Forbidden (CROSS_SCOPE_TAINT_CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED)\n```\n\n![Agent Kill Switch and Immediate Session Revocation](images/18-agent-kill-switch.png)\n\nWhen an agent reads a resource with an elevated confidentiality label, its session high-water mark increases. Any subsequent write operation to a lower-privilege target is halted immediately with an error requiring human operator confirmation.\n\n---\n\n## STRIDE Threat Mitigation Matrix\n\n| Threat Category | Potential Attack Vector | \ud83d\udc4dkanban Architectural Defense |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Spoofing** | Forged session cookies or stolen API tokens | Multi-Card AAL2 enforcement, cryptographic token binding, and WebAuthn Passkeys. |\n| **Tampering** | Parameter manipulation or state corruption | PostgreSQL immutable audit ledger and Wasm state transition validators. |\n| **Repudiation** | Denying ticket edits or deletions | Cryptographically signed append-only activity ledger recording principal IDs. |\n| **Information Disclosure** | Cross-tenant data leakage or ID enumeration | PostgreSQL FORCE RLS on 100% of tables and uniform 404 anti-enumeration responses. |\n| **Denial of Service** | Deep query recursion or token exhaustion | Strict limit of 16 WebSocket conns per user, query depth caps, and MCP token budgets. |\n| **Elevation of Privilege** | Agent tool escalation or script escape | Sandboxed Wasm/nsjail execution and two-stage destructive confirmation tokens. |\n"
+    "content": "# [Informational] Zero-Trust Security Architecture and Explicit Access Visibility\n\n## Overview\n\nSecurity is Priority 0 in \ud83d\udc4dkanban. Rather than relying on fragile application-level checks or leaky implicit permissions, \ud83d\udc4dkanban implements an uncompromising zero-trust architecture. Every request, query, and agent invocation is authenticated, scoped, and strictly evaluated against mathematical access invariants.\n\nKey security pillars:\n1. **Hardware-Enforced Database RLS:** PostgreSQL `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` is enabled across 100% of tenant tables.\n2. **Zero-Context Fail-Closed Execution:** Unbound database sessions return zero rows.\n3. **Explicit Access Visibility:** Every ticket, doc, and sticky renders an explicit sharing manifest with zero cross-tenant enumeration.\n4. **Agent On-Behalf-Of (OBO) Key Scoping:** Agent permissions are computed strictly as the mathematical intersection of the calling user's permissions and the API key's scope.\n5. **MCP Read-Taint High-Water Mark Defense:** Prevents autonomous agents from exfiltrating sensitive data read from higher-privilege scopes into lower-privilege write targets.\n\n---\n\n## Universal Access Control Model\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban unifies human users, user groups, and autonomous agent service-principals under a single Universal Object Graph access model.\n\n### Effective Resource Access (ERA) Evaluation\n\nAccess to any resource (`Project`, `Issue`, `Doc`, `Sticky`, `View`) is determined dynamically:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    User[\"Principal (Human / Agent)\"] --> Inherited[\"Inherited RBAC (Workspace & Project Roles)\"]\n    User --> Direct[\"Direct Resource ACLs (Allow & Deny Masks)\"]\n    Inherited --> Combined[\"Effective User Access = (Inherited \u222a Allow) \\ Deny\"]\n    \n    subgraph AgentOBO[\"Agent On-Behalf-Of Clamping\"]\n        Combined --> Intersect{\"Intersection (\u2229)\"}\n        KeyScope[\"API Key Scope Constraints<br/>(Allowed surfaces, projects, tools)\"] --> Intersect\n        AgentGrant[\"Explicit Agent Grant\"] --> Intersect\n        Intersect --> Effective[\"Final Effective Agent Permission\"]\n    end\n```\n\nWhere:\n- **Roles:** `Owner`, `Admin`, `Member`, `Guest`.\n- **Authoritative 8-Bit Capability Bitmask (`Capability(IntFlag)`):**\n  * `VIEW` (`0x01` / `1`): Read ticket summary, description, and metadata.\n  * `COMMENT` (`0x02` / `2`): Add comments to discussions and activity feeds.\n  * `EDIT` (`0x04` / `4`): Mutate scalar fields, tags, and assignments.\n  * `TRANSITION` (`0x08` / `8`): Move card state across workflow stages.\n  * `LINK` (`0x10` / `16`): Create or sever typed object links.\n  * `SHARE` (`0x20` / `32`): Modify access audience and direct object sharing.\n  * `ADMIN` (`0x40` / `64`): Manage permissions, scripted fields, and audit settings.\n  * `DELETE` (`0x80` / `128`): Soft-trash or permanently purge entities.\n  * `ALL` (`0xFF` / `255`): Full administrative capability mask.\n- **`SCOPE_CAPABILITY_MAP`:** Maps OAuth/API key scopes to capabilities:\n  * `session`: `Capability.ALL` (`0xFF`)\n  * `read`, `agent:read`, `docs:read`, `admin:read`: `Capability.VIEW` (`0x01`)\n  * `comment`: `Capability.VIEW | Capability.COMMENT` (`0x03`)\n  * `write`: `Capability.VIEW | Capability.COMMENT | Capability.EDIT | Capability.TRANSITION | Capability.LINK` (`0x1F`)\n  * `agent:write`: `Capability.VIEW | Capability.COMMENT | Capability.EDIT | Capability.TRANSITION | Capability.LINK` (`0x1F`)\n  * `docs:write`: `Capability.VIEW | Capability.COMMENT | Capability.EDIT | Capability.LINK` (`0x17`)\n  * `admin:write`: `Capability.ALL` (`0xFF`)\n- **Agent OBO Key Intersection:** When an autonomous agent acts on behalf of a user using an API key, the effective permission is clamped:\n  ```\n  AgentEffectiveAccess = UserPermissions INTERSECT KeyScopeConstraints\n  ```\n  An agent cannot escalate privileges beyond the user who generated the key, nor can it exceed the explicit scope configured on the key.\n\n### Monotonic CredentialBoundary Enforcement\n\nAll issued credentials (MCP API keys, personal user tokens, and OAuth 2.1 access tokens) enforce a strict, monotonic privilege ceiling via the `CredentialBoundary` kernel:\n\n- **Monotonic Clamping:** An issued key can only narrow privileges, never expand them. The effective capability bitmask of any token is computed as:\n  ```\n  TokenEffectiveCapability = IssuerActiveCapability BITWISE-AND KeyCapabilityMask\n  ```\n- **Read-Only Scoping:** When minting a read-only token (`capability_mask = 1`, `VIEW` only), the system emits strictly read-only scopes (`${surface}:read`). Write scopes (`${surface}:write`) are completely withheld. Any incoming API or MCP invocation carrying a write payload is rejected at the route gateway before reaching application logic.\n- **Allowed CIDRs (Optional Network Pinning):** Administrators and users can bind keys to explicit IPv4 or IPv6 CIDR subnets (e.g., `192.168.1.0/24, 2001:db8::/32`). Invocations from client IP addresses outside the allowlist receive an immediate `403 Forbidden` (`FORBIDDEN_CIDR`).\n\n---\n\n## PostgreSQL FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY (100% Coverage)\n\nEvery tenant table in PostgreSQL enforces Row-Level Security:\n\n```sql\nALTER TABLE issues ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nALTER TABLE issues FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\n```\n\n### The Fail-Closed Context Invariant\n\nPostgreSQL sessions authenticate as the unprivileged database role `kanban_app`. This role is stripped of `SUPERUSER` and `BYPASSRLS`. To query or mutate tenant data, the application server must establish an explicit session context within a transaction:\n\n```sql\nSET LOCAL app.current_principal_id = '01932e6b-7341-789a-bc01-123456789abc';\nSET LOCAL app.current_workspace_id = '01932e6b-7341-789a-bc01-def012345678';\n```\n\nIf a developer writes a raw query without setting the session context, the RLS policies fail closed: **zero rows are returned, and zero mutations succeed**. Even if an attacker executes SQL injection, they cannot bypass RLS to read cross-tenant data.\n\n---\n\n## Explicit Access Visibility and Sharing Manifests\n\nSaaS platforms often hide sharing rules behind obscure settings menus, leading to accidental leaks. In \ud83d\udc4dkanban, every resource view exposes a dynamic `\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f` sharing pill.\n\nClicking the sharing pill reveals the **Access Manifest**:\n- **Explicit Principals:** Lists all users, groups, and autonomous agents with direct access.\n- **Inherited Scope:** Displays parent project and workspace inheritance paths.\n- **Auditable Effective Mask:** Shows exact read, write, share, and delete capabilities for each principal.\n\n![Access Visibility Inspector and Sharing Manifest](images/17-access-visibility-inspector.png)\n\n### Anti-Enumeration and Timing Defense\n\nTo prevent malicious actors from scanning IDs or usernames:\n- **Identical RFC 7807 404 Responses:** Accessing an unauthorized resource returns an identical `404 Not Found` response as a non-existent resource. Unauthorized users cannot determine whether an object exists.\n- **25ms Timing Floor:** All resource lookups enforce a synthetic 25ms timing floor to neutralize side-channel timing attacks that measure database index lookups.\n\n---\n\n## MCP Read-Taint High-Water Mark Defense\n\nAutonomous LLMs can be tricked by prompt injection into reading confidential documents and writing excerpts into public tickets. \ud83d\udc4dkanban prevents this through **Read-Taint Tracking**:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Agent as Autonomous Agent\n    participant Gateway as MCP Gateway Taint Engine\n    participant PrivateDoc as Confidential Doc (Tier: PRIVATE)\n    participant PublicTicket as Public Ticket (Tier: PUBLIC)\n\n    Agent->>Gateway: read_doc(id='confidential_spec')\n    Gateway->>PrivateDoc: Fetch Document\n    PrivateDoc-->>Gateway: Return Content\n    Gateway->>Gateway: Elevate Agent Session High-Water Mark to PRIVATE\n    Gateway-->>Agent: Deliver Markdown Payload\n\n    Note over Agent: Later in conversation...\n    Agent->>Gateway: update_ticket(id='public_bug', body=...)\n    Gateway->>Gateway: Check Destination Scope (PUBLIC) vs High-Water Mark (PRIVATE)\n    Gateway-->>Agent: 403 Forbidden (CROSS_SCOPE_TAINT_CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED)\n```\n\n![Agent Kill Switch and Immediate Session Revocation](images/18-agent-kill-switch.png)\n\nWhen an agent reads a resource with an elevated confidentiality label, its session high-water mark increases. Any subsequent write operation to a lower-privilege target is halted immediately with an error requiring human operator confirmation.\n\n---\n\n## Distributed Runner Dedicated mTLS Port (:8444) and PoP Handshake\n\nRemote execution daemons and test runners never share general HTTP application ingress. Instead, they authenticate through a dedicated mutual TLS port:\n\n- **Dedicated Port `:8444`:** Distributed runners communicate exclusively with the orchestrator over port `:8444`. Port `:8921` handles human web traffic and general REST/MCP calls, while `:8444` enforces mandatory client certificate exchange.\n- **Cryptographic Proof-of-Possession (PoP):** During runner enrollment, the daemon generates a local private key and presents a hardware-bound CSR. Upon successful verification of the short-lived enrollment token (15-minute TTL), the orchestrator signs the client certificate. Every subsequent WebSocket connection requires a cryptographic Proof-of-Possession challenge, verifying the daemon possesses the matching private key before receiving build workloads.\n- **Hardware-Bound Certificate Rotation:** Certificates cannot be silently renewed. If a runner certificate expires or requires rotation, the administrator revokes the runner identity in `/admin/agent-runners` (requiring AAL2 step-up verification), mints a fresh enrollment token, and re-enrolls the runner host.\n\n---\n\n## Private-Only Internal MCP Route Isolation (/internal/v1/mcp/*)\n\nInternal agent orchestration, runner dispatch, and distributed tool calls are isolated from external edge routing:\n\n- **Zero Edge Routing:** All `/internal/v1/mcp/*` endpoints are bound strictly to internal container networks (`app_net`, `data_net`). Reverse proxies (`edge_net`) explicitly drop or block `/internal/*` request paths from external ingress.\n- **mTLS Verification:** Calls across internal MCP routes require valid internal mTLS certificates or cryptographic service-principal tokens signed by the local authority. Unauthorized external callers cannot reach or probe internal agent pipelines.\n\n---\n\n## STRIDE Threat Mitigation Matrix\n\n| Threat Category | Potential Attack Vector | \ud83d\udc4dkanban Architectural Defense |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Spoofing** | Forged session cookies or stolen API tokens | Multi-Card AAL2 enforcement, cryptographic token binding, dedicated `:8444` mTLS PoP, and WebAuthn Passkeys. |\n| **Tampering** | Parameter manipulation or state corruption | PostgreSQL immutable audit ledger and Wasm state transition validators. |\n| **Repudiation** | Denying ticket edits or deletions | Cryptographically signed append-only activity ledger recording principal IDs. |\n| **Information Disclosure** | Cross-tenant data leakage or ID enumeration | PostgreSQL FORCE RLS on 100% of tables, `/internal/v1/mcp/*` edge isolation, and uniform 404 anti-enumeration responses. |\n| **Denial of Service** | Deep query recursion or token exhaustion | Strict limit of 16 WebSocket conns per user, query depth caps, CIDR filtering, and MCP token budgets. |\n| **Elevation of Privilege** | Agent tool escalation or script escape | Sandboxed Wasm/nsjail execution, monotonic `CredentialBoundary` ceiling, and two-stage destructive confirmation tokens. |\n"
   },
